<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karabakh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qarabagh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karabakh is a region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>, between the Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> mountains and the Kura and Aras rivers, today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Its highlands, long home to Armenian princes and meliks, and its lowland pastures were ruled in turn by Persian, Turkic and local dynasties, then by the Karabakh Khanate and, from 1805, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It was fought over in 1988–1994 and 2020, and Azerbaijan took control of the whole region in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karabakh.jpg" alt="Karabakh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LJ user plusninety · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karmir-shuka.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karabakh extends from the highlands of the Lesser Caucasus down to the lowlands between the rivers Kura and Aras. It is usually divided into Highland Karabakh, Lowland Karabakh (the steppes between the two rivers) and the eastern slopes of the Zangezur Mountains. No official designation fixes the whole of its extent. Its widest historical bounds are those of the Karabakh Khanate of the 18th century, which ran from the Zangezur Mountains along the Aras to its meeting with the Kura, and north along the Kura as far as what is now the Mingachevir reservoir.</p><p>The name Karabakh is first recorded in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries in Georgian and Persian sources, and became common after the Mongol conquest of the 1230s. It is generally believed to combine the Turkic kara, black, with the Iranian bagh, garden, though other explanations have been proposed, including kara in the sense of large. Place names in the region form several layers, Turkic, Armenian, Iranian and Russian, and for centuries sedentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and nomadic Turkic and Kurdish herders used two overlapping sets of names for the same places.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region is believed to have been conquered by the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the 2nd century BCE and organised as parts of the provinces of Artsakh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utik">Utik</a> and southern Syunik. After the partition of Armenia by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 387 CE, Artsakh and Utik became part of the Caucasian Albanian satrapy of Sassanian Persia. After the Arab invasions several Armenian princes established their dominance in the highlands, ruling as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> through the House of Khachen and later the Melikdoms of Karabakh. From the 11th century Oghuz Turkic tribes settled the region and moved between winter pastures in the lowlands and summer pastures in the highlands.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a>, the Mongol Ilkhanate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirid Sultanates</a>, the Qara Qoyunlu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and Safavid Iran held the highlands in turn between 821 and the early nineteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ruled Karabakh in 1578–1605 and again in 1723–1736. In 1747 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panah-ali-khan">Panah Ali Khan</a>, a chieftain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanshir">Javanshir</a> clan, seized control after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> and founded the Karabakh Khanate. Qajar Iran re-established its rule several years later, and the region then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> as its most prominent city.</p><p>After 1917 Karabakh was disputed between the new republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and in 1921 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> decided it would remain within Soviet Azerbaijan. In 1923 the Armenian-inhabited highlands became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh-autonomous-oblast">Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast</a>. In February 1988 its Supreme Soviet voted to unite with Armenia, and a war followed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s collapse. A further war in 2020 ended with a ceasefire signed on 10 November 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karabakh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…November 1467 he surprised and killed the Qara Qoyunlu ruler Jahan Shah in eastern Anatolia, and in 1469 he trapped the Timurid sultan Abu Sa’id, who had invaded Azerbaijan, by cutting his supplies during the winter in the <b>Qarabagh</b> plain; Abu Sa’id was captured and put to death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Levon Ter-Petrosyan and his defence minister Vazgen Sargsyan led Armenia through the First Nagorno-Karabakh War, which the <b>Karabakh</b> Armenian forces won, taking practically the whole former autonomous oblast and most of seven adjacent districts of Azerbaijan before the Russian-brokered ceasefire of 1994; an estimated 30,000 people had been…</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Moscow's indecision on <b>Karabakh</b>, the slow relief after the earthquake and the failing economy widened the gap between Yerevan and the centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1797 he was assassinated by his servants at Shusha in <b>Karabakh</b>, and his nephew Baba Khan succeeded him as Fath Ali Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Baku's oil was its economic base, but it faced conflicts with Armenia over <b>Karabakh</b> and Zangezur.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-democratic-republic">Azerbaijan Democratic Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nader Shah's death in 1747, Shahverdi Khan of the Ziyadoghlu branch of the Qajar tribe, whose family had long governed Ganja and <b>Karabakh</b> for the Safavids, made Ganja an independent khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
